4. Spring @Transaction Internal Working
       When you annotate a method with @Transactional, Spring doesn’t change your method itself. Instead, it creates a
       proxy object that wraps your original bean.

       When you call transactional method from outside the class, Spring routes the call through a proxy, which:
           Starts a transaction
           Invokes the original method
           Commits or rolls back
       Key Components Involved
           @Transactional	Declarative marker
           TransactionInterceptor	Core AOP logic
           PlatformTransactionManager	Starts, commits, rolls back transactions
           DataSourceTransactionManager	JDBC transactions (for RDBMS)
           JpaTransactionManager	JPA/Hibernate transactions

      [Client Code]
            ↓
      [Spring Proxy] ← @Transactional logic (starts tx)
            ↓
      [Target Method] ← Your actual logic
            ↓
      [Spring Proxy] ← commits or rolls back

