25. Why HashMap’s load factor is 0.75?
    - Collision vs. Memory Trade-off
      • A lower load factor (e.g. 0.5) reduces collisions but wastes more memory.
      • A higher load factor (e.g. 0.9) uses memory more tightly but increases collisions and lookup time.
    - Amortized Resizing Cost
      • At 0.75, you minimize the number of expensive resize-and-rehash operations while keeping average bucket chain/tree lengths short.
    - Empirical Sweet Spot
      • In practice, 0.75 gives you good overall performance (O(1) average lookup) without excessive memory overhead.

