31. Sealed Classes help us restrict which classes can extend or implement a superclass or interface. Sealed Classes are
beneficial in cases when we need to control the classes which can implement or extend them

    public abstract sealed class Shape permits Circle, Rectangle, Square {
        public abstract int getNumberOfSides();
    }
    public final class Circle extends Shape {
        public int getNumberOfSides() { return 0; }
    }
    public non-sealed class Rectangle extends Shape {
        public int getNumberOfSides() { return 4; }
    }
    public final class Square extends Rectangle {
        public int getNumberOfSides() { return 4; }
    }

